---
/*
 * Upgrading (chapter 10). The whole of this page was an <h3> called
 * "Upgrading later" inside step 1 of Getting started until 2026-09-14,
 * sitting between `cargo install shep` and writing a Flockfile: 496 words
 * on daemon reloads, handover and protocol versions, in front of a reader
 * who had not yet installed anything. It was 2.2 times the length of that
 * page's entire happy path.
 *
 * Nothing here is rewritten. The prose is the prose that was there, split
 * across five headings where it already changed subject, and given a lede.
 */
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import ReferencePills from "../../components/docs/ReferencePills.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  activeSlug="upgrading"
>
  <article>
    <h1>Upgrading</h1>
    <ReferencePills slug="upgrading" />
    <p class="lede">
      <code>shep upgrade</code> replaces the binary through the channel shep
      came from, reloads the shepherd, then upgrades the dogs. The binary on
      disk changes nothing that is already running.{" "}
      <code>shep daemon reload</code> is what moves a live flock onto it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="replacing-the-binary">Replacing the binary</h2>
    <div class="terminal">
      <div><span class="prompt">$</span> shep upgrade</div>
    </div>
    <p>
      shep lists what is newer, asks once, and runs the channel's own command
      in the foreground. Which command depends on how shep was installed:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Install channel</th><th>Command shep runs</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Homebrew</td><td data-label="Command shep runs"><code>brew upgrade shep</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>cargo</td><td data-label="Command shep runs"><code>cargo install --locked shep</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Scoop</td><td data-label="Command shep runs"><code>scoop update shep</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>winget</td><td data-label="Command shep runs"><code>winget upgrade shep-pm.shep</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Chocolatey</td><td data-label="Command shep runs"><code>choco upgrade shep</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>A <code>.deb</code> or an archive</td><td data-label="Command shep runs">none: shep prints the steps</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="the-reload-question">The reload question</h3>
    <p>
      Once the new shep is on <code>PATH</code>, a shepherd that is running
      gets one question on a terminal:{" "}
      <code>Reload the shepherd onto 0.25.0 now? [Y/n]</code>. Enter says yes.{" "}
      <code>--reload</code> answers it in advance. With no terminal to ask,
      shep reloads nothing and prints <code>shep daemon reload</code> for you
      to run. The dogs are upgraded either way.
    </p>

    <h3 id="on-windows">On Windows</h3>
    <p>
      The shepherd holds <code>shep.exe</code>, so shep does not run the
      command. It prints it, with <code>shep daemon reload</code> to run
      after. Windows cannot hand the flock over, so the flock restarts on
      that reload.
    </p>

    <h3 id="replacing-by-hand">Replacing the binary by hand</h3>
    <p>
      A channel shep cannot run gets steps instead. For a <code>.deb</code>,
      download it from the latest release, check it against{" "}
      <code>SHA256SUMS</code> and install it with{" "}
      <code>sudo dpkg -i shep_*.deb</code>. For an archive, download the one
      for your target, check it the same way and put the shep inside it where
      the old one is. By hand, the shepherd keeps the code it booted with
      until you reload it, and each dog keeps its own until you upgrade it.
    </p>
    <div class="terminal">
      <div><span class="prompt">$</span> shep upgrade<span class="muted"> # or, step by step:</span></div>
      <div><span class="prompt">$</span> cargo install --locked shep</div>
      <div><span class="prompt">$</span> shep upgrade --dogs<span class="muted"> # every installed dog</span></div>
      <div><span class="prompt">$</span> shep daemon reload</div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="what-carries-across">What carries across</h2>
    <p>
      A flock that can be carried keeps running through the reload. The
      shepherd replaces its own image in place, so every sheep keeps the pid
      it already had and its log carries on where it was. The dogs cross
      with it rather than being stopped and started alongside the flock; the
      dogs page says what each one does on the far side. One that cannot be
      carried is
      stopped and started instead, which moves every pid and starts every log
      again; <a href="#when-it-cannot-carry">the next section</a> says which
      flocks those are.
    </p>
    <p>
      If a dog cannot talk to the new shepherd, the reload names it. It waits
      for the dogs to reconnect first, so the answer is about the shepherd
      now running, not the one it replaced.
    </p>

    <p>
      Listing the built-in dogs in <code>shep flock</code> never costs a
      sheep. One you already named <code>metrics</code> or <code>bark</code>{" "}
      keeps its name and its id. The shepherd leaves that dog out, warns at
      boot to rename one of the two, and restores the sheep even when{" "}
      <code>[daemon] boot_first_dogs</code> names the dog.
    </p>

    <h2 id="when-it-cannot-carry">When it cannot carry</h2>
    <p class="fine">
      One thing still sends a reload down the older path: a sheep whose log
      pump has stopped answering. The shepherd cannot say which files that
      sheep is writing to, so it stops and starts the flock instead and
      names the sheep that held it back. Coming from 0.1.17 or earlier, the
      first reload always takes that path too: the shepherd being replaced
      predates the handover and has nothing to hand over with.
    </p>

    <h2 id="reload-reads-the-config-too">A reload re-reads the config too</h2>
    <p>
      The reload re-reads <code>shep.toml</code> too, and always has. So it
      is also the command for a changed log level, a new socket path or an
      edited <code>[interpreters]</code> table, with no new binary involved.
      It is a different verb from <code>shep reload &lt;sheep&gt;</code>,
      which replaces one sheep's processes and reads no file at all: the
      shepherd's own config and an app's Flockfile are two separate things
      that happen to share a word.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      It re-reads <code>enabled_dogs</code> with it. A dog the new file no
      longer names is stopped and marked <code>disabled</code>, running or
      already stopped, and one already parked is registered at rest under a
      new id.
    </p>

    <h2 id="version-skew">Version skew</h2>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      Skip the reload that <code>shep upgrade</code> offers (or{" "}
      <code>shep daemon reload</code> by hand) and the new binary answers{" "}
      <code>shep --version</code> while the running shepherd is still the
      old one. <code>flock</code>, <code>describe</code> and{" "}
      <code>bleats</code> only warn. <code>kill</code> and{" "}
      <code>ping</code> always work. Every other verb refuses and names{" "}
      <code>shep daemon reload</code>.
    </Callout>
    <p class="fine">
      Over <code>--host</code> the check is the protocol, not the version: an
      older shep on the same protocol is written to, with one{" "}
      <code>version_skew</code> notice. Another protocol refuses, naming both
      numbers and the fix on whichever side is behind: a reload over ssh for
      a host behind this build, the install channel's own command here for one
      ahead.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      An older <code>shep</code> re-running{" "}
      <code>shep start &lt;Flockfile&gt;</code> gets the shepherd's refusal on
      a disabled sheep's row and stops there, so the rest of the file does
      not start.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>shep start &lt;Flockfile&gt;</code> used to be the one verb that
      did not refuse cleanly across that skew: applying a Flockfile is
      additive, so the number did not move, and the handshake passed while
      the shepherd then ended the connection on an envelope it could not
      read. It refuses cleanly now, because the <code>--reset</code> flag's
      own wire spelling changed, which is not additive. The number has moved
      twice more since, both for additive changes. Reloading first is still
      the whole fix.
    </p>

    <h2 id="going-back">Going back to an older version</h2>
    <p class="fine">
      An older <code>shep</code> reads no <a href="/docs/lifecycle">disabled</a>
      marks out of a handover blob or a flock file. A parked sheep stays down
      and loses its guard: <code>start</code>, <code>restart</code> and
      <code>stock</code> stop refusing it, a sweep or a re-run Flockfile starts
      it, and nothing holds it down after that. <code>shep flock</code> stops
      saying <code>disabled</code>. Run <code>shep disable</code> again after
      going back.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .terminal {
    font-family: "Space Mono", monospace;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.85;
    background: var(--code-bg);
    border: 3px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 14px;
    padding: 16px 18px;
    margin: 0 0 26px;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .prompt {
    color: var(--ink-3);
    margin-right: 8px;
  }

  .muted {
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
</style>
